index 4479b53..e4395be 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -2,6 +2,18 @@
 
 This project is a sample news application built with Astro, designed to serve as a testing ground for new features in Chrome DevTools.
 
+## Account settings and passkeys
+
+Use Node.js 22.13 or newer. Password sign-in is verified by the existing Times identity service; the app then rotates its HTTP-only Astro session and grants an eight-hour authenticated session. Account settings are available at `/devtools-times/account/settings` after sign-in. Passkey management requests require that session, and mutations also require a matching Origin header. Removing the last passkey is allowed because password sign-in remains available.
+
+The identity service does not expose passkey storage endpoints. This app stores verified registrations and their labels in SQLite at `data/accounts.sqlite`, independent of browser sessions. Set `ACCOUNT_DB_PATH` to a persistent, private volume in production. All processes must use the same database; deployments across separate machines need shared database storage instead of independent SQLite files. Existing credentials from another service require an explicit migration including their original user handles, public keys, and RP IDs; the app does not invent or seed credentials.
+
+`WEBAUTHN_RP_ID` optionally pins the relying party ID; otherwise the request hostname is used. Use HTTPS in production and keep the RP ID stable. Registration verifies the session-bound challenge, origin, RP ID, and user verification using SimpleWebAuthn. Passkeys registered here are listed for the current RP ID only. Password sign-in remains the existing authentication method; this change does not add a passkey sign-in ceremony.
+
+The management section supports creation, listing, renaming, and confirmed removal. Nicknames are account labels, separate from account profile names and password-manager labels. Supported browsers receive `signalAllAcceptedCredentials` on load and after removal, including an empty list when the last key is removed. Unsupported browsers retain full server management and show manual cleanup guidance. Sync failures do not undo completed changes and can be retried. Dates come from stored timestamps; unused credentials say “Not used yet.”
+
+Provider names and selected light icons in `src/utils/aaguids.json` come from the [passkey-authenticator-aaguids registry](https://github.com/passkeydeveloper/passkey-authenticator-aaguids). They are display hints only, with a neutral fallback for zero or unrecognized AAGUIDs. The registry is local so viewing account settings does not send account activity to a metadata service.
+
 
 ```sh
 pnpm install && pnpm run start
